{:edb/db-before-t 1322 :edb/db-after-t 1323 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534635 50 #inst "2026-10-08T03:02:18.430Z" 13194139534635 true] [17592186078325 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300041 13194139534635 true] [17592186078405 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300034 13194139534635 true] [17592186078406 1020 #edb/ref 17592186300048 13194139534635 true] [17592186078906 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534635 true] [17592186078906 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3027743251b98968805564e296d7570d80d35ff64b27cf831e631ed9c8efb40a.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y=f(x)$ above continuous?" 13194139534635 true] [17592186078906 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48238.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $y = f(x)$ above continuous?" 13194139534635 false] [17592186078906 1145 "The function is continuous on the open intervals $(-3,0)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is right-continuous at $x=-3$ and left-continuous at $x=0.$ Hence, it is continuous on the closed interval $[-3,0].$\n\nTherefore, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $[-3,0]$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nAmong the answer choices, the correct one is the following:\n\n$\\,$ $[-3,0]$ \n\nNotice that:\n\n- $[0,1]$ and $[0,4]$ contain the endpoint $x=0,$ but the function is not right-continuous at this point.\n\n- $[-1,1]$ and $[-2,2]$ contain the point of discontinuity $x=0,$ so the function is not continuous on these intervals." 13194139534635 true] [17592186078909 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534635 true] [17592186078909 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/09d006953b4fc1d111a84e78fcc6b2038d53768fb82d6f61a0b2a1d0126bbb40.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous? \n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,0]$\n- $x\\in [-3,2]$\n- $x\\in (0,\\infty )$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139534635 true] [17592186078909 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/612/Source/Images/q-48344.png)\n\nConsider the function $y = f(x)$ above. On which of the following intervals is the function continuous?\n\n1. $[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 0]]$\n2. $x ∈ [- 3, 2]$\n3. $x ∈ (0, ∞)$\n\nYou may assume that all points of discontinuity of $f(x)$ are shown in the diagram." 13194139534635 false] [17592186078909 1145 "Let's examine each statement.\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(-\\infty ,0]$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=-3.$\n\n- The function is continuous on $[-3,2].$ It is continuous on $(-3,2),$ and also right continuous at $x=-3$ and left continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- The function is *not* continuous on $(0,\\infty )$ because it contains a discontinuity at $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300034 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300035 13194139534635 true] [17592186300034 1089 "q-48279" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300034 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534635 true] [17592186300034 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(1,3]?$" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300034 1113 #uuid "0790ffcc-e651-53c9-a00a-a9cbd12d21cb" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300034 1145 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png)\n\nIndeed, we have that $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $(1,3].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$, $h(x)$,  and $q(x)$ are not right-continuous at $x=3.$\n\n- $p(x)$ is not continuous at  $x=2$, and so is not continuous on $(1,3].$" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300036 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300037 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300038 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300039 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300040 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1041 "selection" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1104 #edb/ref 17592186300036 13194139534635 true] [17592186300035 1110 #uuid "c4de47f3-3670-52cc-9337-ef541d36c72a" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300036 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c24d4207dc777027919d211e4b2262d7340caf25a4166fa004e6e8129da5614.png" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300036 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534635 true] [17592186300036 1111 #uuid "96ea7e20-13e0-5802-a213-2cdefded01e4" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300037 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/387ed50e8983c83f96ea03bdd7a47d13d33a0c6e0728b52512fef8296351f73e.png" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300037 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534635 true] [17592186300037 1111 #uuid "6bb18bc0-9a99-5855-b7c6-0f74a53c2b27" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300038 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/895c64a995d7f07a161a43945c369395b5cb365c5e7ec0f664df130c49795af8.png" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300038 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534635 true] [17592186300038 1111 #uuid "71a8cbbb-31f6-54c3-8cc7-59c26a538c4c" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300039 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/7f2b4da10350a6ba230e0ec1b0b26822b133ee4a9c1228a61565ed559efcdd64.png" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300039 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534635 true] [17592186300039 1111 #uuid "4bd931eb-aff4-51b2-bf97-e00df57b9670" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300040 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/3c5fedec0f96ef81c33146504cdabc8f0400ba79f77591167a285d119d238fd2.png" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300040 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139534635 true] [17592186300040 1111 #uuid "45008099-0a9a-5bef-82a2-0f66e7d2dce8" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300041 1017 #edb/ref 17592186300042 13194139534635 true] [17592186300041 1089 "q-76091" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300041 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534635 true] [17592186300041 1100 "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $(-2,\\infty )?$ You may assume that all points of discontinuity for each function are shown." 13194139534635 true] [17592186300041 1113 #uuid "b8055c33-18c1-54b4-a55d-b97e3d2f96f4" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300041 1145 "The correct answer is the following.\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981048/assets/c6cfdc41036d1dc319054c0cf397276f40faa2c749ea59820b45875cb31b3d9f.png)\n\n    \n\nIndeed, we have that $h(x)$ is continuous on the open interval $(-2,\\infty ).$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $q(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not continuous at $x=1,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ),$\n\n- $g(x)$is not continuous at $x=2,$ but this point lies within the interval $(-2,\\infty ).$" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300043 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300044 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300045 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300046 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1000 #edb/ref 17592186300047 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1041 "selection" 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1104 #edb/ref 17592186300044 13194139534635 true] [17592186300042 1110 #uuid "a2565675-69c4-5459-9308-0049091